Post-Viral Autonomic and Cardiovascular Dysregulations
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Postural tachycardia >30 bpm, Orthostatic pre-syncope, Severe post-exertional malaise

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-037
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe pre-syncope, palpitations, and inability to stand following a recent viral illness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe lightheadedness, a racing heart, and near-fainting every time the patient attempts to stand, rendering them completely bedbound. Immediate assessment is required to rule out acute cardiac arrhythmias, pulmonary embolism, or severe dehydration before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every time I try to get out of bed, my heart pounds out of my chest, the room goes black, and I have to lie back down before I pass out."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the onset of severe orthostatic intolerance and autonomic instability directly following a documented viral infection (suspected COVID-19) four weeks ago. The primary symptom is a profound postural tachycardia, distinctly characterized by a subjective feeling of a racing heart immediately upon standing. This is accompanied by severe pre-syncopal symptoms including lightheadedness, palpitations, and blurred vision during the orthostatic challenge. The patient reports extreme, debilitating post-exertional malaise entirely disproportionate to the level of physical exertion; walking to the bathroom necessitates hours of rest. There is prominent brain fog featuring severe concentration difficulties. The patient also notes gastrointestinal dysmotility manifesting as severe nausea and unprovoked bloating.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound due to severe orthostatic intolerance.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of syncope and traumatic falls if attempting to mobilize to the bathroom.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg (Supine), 108/68 mmHg (Standing 3 mins) | HR: 72 bpm (Supine), 115 bpm (Standing 3 mins) | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Pale, anxious, lying completely flat in bed.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular examination reveals a distinct lack of structural cardiac pathology (normal S1/S2, no murmurs, JVP not elevated). Active stand test (poor man's tilt table) demonstrates a resting supine heart rate of 72 bpm, which rapidly escalates to 115 bpm within 3 minutes of standing, without a significant drop in blood pressure (ruling out classic orthostatic hypotension). The patient becomes visibly pale and complains of severe pre-syncope, necessitating an immediate return to the supine position. Chest is clear. Abdomen is soft and non-tender. Neurological exam shows no focal deficits, but patient is slow to answer questions (brain fog).
Environmental Observations: Multiple water bottles by the bed, completely untouched due to nausea.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Viral Autonomic Dysregulation (Suspected Postural Orthostatic Tachycardia Syndrome - POTS)
Differentials:

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lungs, normal oxygen saturation, absence of pleuritic chest pain, and the tachycardia is strictly postural rather than constant at rest.

Classic Orthostatic Hypotension (e.g., severe dehydration) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a >20 mmHg drop in systolic blood pressure upon standing, despite the massive >30 bpm spike in heart rate.

Paroxysmal Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual (sinus) escalation of heart rate explicitly tied to standing, and immediate resolution upon lying flat, rather than sudden on/off electrical circuits.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, debilitating post-viral autonomic instability, specifically profound postural tachycardia and pre-syncope preventing all basic mobilization. The dramatic heart rate spike and near-fainting triggered an after-hours call due to fears of an acute cardiac event. Delaying assessment would result in severe anxiety, total functional immobility, and high risk of a syncopal fall. Urgent clinical evaluation definitively ruled out structural cardiac emergencies, pulmonary embolism, and classic hypovolemic shock, confirming a severe dysautonomic state. This permitted the immediate initiation of strict positional, hydration, and counter-maneuver strategies to safely restore basic mobility, fulfilling MBS criteria for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered 1L Normal Saline IV over 2 hours on-site to rapidly expand intravascular volume and blunt the orthostatic reflex. Advised aggressive oral fluid (3L/day) and salt loading.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Step 1: By drinking 500mL of cold water rapidly before getting out of bed, the patient actively triggers the body's natural reflex to constrict blood vessels, immediately preventing the blood from pooling in the legs upon standing.

Step 2: By tightly crossing the legs and squeezing the thigh and calf muscles before standing up, the patient physically pumps the blood back up to the heart, directly stopping the extreme heart rate spike and dizziness.

Step 3: By showering sitting down on a stool with lukewarm water, the patient avoids the extreme blood vessel dilation caused by hot water and standing, completely preventing a fainting episode in the bathroom.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Shower stool recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to consider beta-blockers or Ivabradine if conservative measures fail.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department if you experience severe, crushing chest pain, sudden severe shortness of breath while lying flat, or if you completely lose consciousness (faint) despite using the leg-squeezing techniques.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Formal diagnosis of POTS requires a Tilt Table Test; this is a presumptive clinical diagnosis based on the active stand test.
Clinical Course: The IV fluids will provide immediate relief for 24-48 hours. The condition often improves slowly over many months. Aggressive hydration and salt intake are the absolute mainstays of daily management.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for review if the nausea prevents you from keeping any fluids down for more than 12 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The recent virus damaged the automatic nervous system that controls your blood vessels. When you stand up, your blood vessels fail to tighten, so blood drops to your legs. Your heart is forced to beat violently fast to pump it back up to your brain to stop you from fainting. IV fluids and salt give your body more blood volume to work with.
Actionable Steps: Drink 3 liters of salty fluids daily. Squeeze leg muscles tight before standing. Sit down immediately if you feel dizzy.

